            "Serpentine refers to a group of hydrated magnesium silicates, mainly antigorite, lizardite and chrysotile. It forms by serpentinisation: seawater or hot fluids penetrate mantle rocks, olivine-rich peridotites, at mid-ocean ridges and subduction zones, and deeply hydrate them. These green rocks, called serpentinites, are later uplifted into mountain belts. Noble serpentine, translucent and yellowish green, is called “bowenite” or “williamsite” depending on the variety.",
            "Monoclinic system, in fine aggregates: platy (antigorite), massive (lizardite) or fibrous (chrysotile, a form of asbestos). No cleavage visible in masses; splintery to conchoidal fracture. Hardness 2.5 to 5.5 depending on the variety, with bowenite reaching 5.5; specific gravity 2.44 to 2.62; greasy to waxy or silky lustre; refractive index 1.55 to 1.57. Pale to dark green, often spotted black (magnetite). Often sold as “new jade”, it is distinguished by its lower hardness and specific gravity. Fibrous chrysotile must not be worked.",
